00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:24,000
Well, as I announced in passing last week, there is a new guide to advancement that replaces the advancement committee policies and procedures, God, and a new Eagle Scout workbook,

51
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:35,000
And I wanted to expand on my first impressions of both of these documents because I think they're going to be tremendous influences for good.

52
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:38,000
You know, nobody really likes change.

53
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:42,000
There have been a couple of pretty substantive changes

54
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:53,000
But you know, as I reviewed the new advancement guide, I wanted to reconcile it with any advice that I've given in the past, because I want to correct any advice to meet whatever new policies and procedures

55
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:54,000
There are.

56
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:58,000
I'm pretty safe on about 99% of them

57
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:06,000
And we'll talk about the couple of things that are different now that I would change my advice to you about.

58
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:12,000
But I think most of what's on the blog and has been in these podcasts is still pretty solid.

59
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:13,000
There's a couple of differences-

60
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:14,000
We'll get to those,

61
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:33,000
But I think it's going to settle a lot of debatable questions and settle a lot of common problems that Scouts have had in trying to advance, by informing us leaders about exactly how this is all to work.

62
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:42,000
One statement in the guide says: the guide to advancement is the official source for administering advancement in all Boy Scouts of America programs.

63
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:52,000
It replaces the advancement committee policies and procedures and advancement and recognition policies and procedures, which are no longer valid

64
00:05:52,000 --> 00:06:04,000
Now I had mentioned earlier in the podcast that there was maybe a date attached to that, but you know that seems to be a pretty definitive statement that upon publication, this is what we're supposed to be doing?

65
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:14,000
I'm going to do my best to revise any former posts at scoutmastercgcom to alert readers to the changes in the new advancement guide.

66
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:18,000
But the guide also adds a very wise statement.

67
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:23,000
It says: be aware that statements or interpretations offered from unofficial websites-

68
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:26,000
I'm an unofficial website- and other such sources-

69
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:31,000
This is an unofficial podcast- maybe out of date or incorrect.

70
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:35,000
It will not be considered in resolving advancement questions and issues

71
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:50,000
In situations not specifically covered in this guide. advancement chairs, coordinators or other administrators should make decisions based on the aims and mission of the Boy Scouts of America, as well as the Scout Oath and Law and Common Sense.

72
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:53,000
How about that?

73
00:06:53,000 --> 00:07:01,000
Here's one thing that that statement tells me and I want to make sure that all of my readers and listeners understand.

74
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:07,000
I don't want to be a source to resolve an argument.

75
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:12,000
If I can point you to the source of resolution, that's fine,

76
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:20,000
But don't take what I offer as opinions to be gospel truth, because it certainly isn't.

77
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:29,000
You should test everything that I say against the resources that are available that state the policies and procedures of the Boy Scouts of America.

78
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:36,000
There's a disclaimer on my website and there's a disclaimer in every podcast that I am not an official voice in any way.

79
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:38,000
I'm just trying to be helpful.

80
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:47,000
So don't consider what is said here or what's written on the blog to be anything other than my opinion,

81
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:54,000
Unless you can back it up with a clearer policy statement or a procedural statement in the BSA literature.

82
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:58,000
So let's make that abundantly clear.

83
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:05,000
Now, in the first couple of pages of the new guide it lists any significant changes

84
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:12,000
And I've reviewed some of these significant changes and I have not reviewed others. but let's just go down the list.

85
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:18,000
Number one: it says Council, District and Union Advancement Committee responsibilities are detailed and listed.

86
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:20,000
I haven't looked at that yet.

87
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:30,000
Number two is that the section on awards and recognitions has been eliminated for integration into a new publication, The Guide to Awards and Insignia.

88
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:40,000
Now I haven't seen that new guide yet, but there's apparently a new publication coming out called The Guide to Awards and Insignia.

89
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:56,000
My imagination tells me that, if what holds true of the new guide to advancement, the new guide to awards and Insignia will probably replace the uniform and Insignia guide that we've had for years and years.

90
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:59,000
Number three: it says Cub Scouting Material updated.

91
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:05,000
I love Cub Scouts but I'm not really actively involved in them, so I haven't looked at that piece.

92
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:07,000
Number four: change number four:

93
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:15,000
Active participation and position of responsibility requirements approached from units established reasonable expectations.

94
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:26,000
Now units can establish some reasonable expectations- with reasonable being the word to emphasize- to active participation and position of responsibility requirements.

95
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:31,000
Now, I've argued against metrics being applied to these types of things in the past.

96
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:39,000
I'm probably going to maintain, in my opinion, that that's really not a great idea. but it is now allowed.

97
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:43,000
It was specifically prohibited by policy, but now it is allowed.

98
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:58,000
This change does allow units to do this- to attach metrics- but it does not allow those metrics alone to be the sole determination of active or to having fulfilled a position of responsibility.

99
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:04,000
Instead, it describes for active, a three-step process and alternatives to that process,

100
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:06,000
And I think I'm going to expand on that.

101
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:15,000
I've already prepared a post for the blog about that and we'll probably expand on that in a future edition of the Scoutmaster podcast.

102
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:20,000
But you may think that I don't like that particular change, but I do.

103
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:25,000
I absolutely support it and I think it's very intelligent and when we get into it you'll see why.

104
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:34,000
Change number five is: they have included venturing and sea scouts in the Guide to Advancement in detail,

105
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:43,000
And I'm sure that that is a great relief to people who are working in those programs to have that easily at hand and to be included in the guide.

106
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:48,000
Number six- and I haven't really looked at this: the Merit Badge section has been reorganized and expanded.

107
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:56,000
Number seven: Board of Review practices clarified, including wearing the uniform, and I have looked at that

108
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:59,000
And again, that will be something we'll expand on later.

109
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:11,000
If you want the short story. nothing really changed there but they clarified the fact that you know you cannot ding a scout for showing up to a Board of Review without a uniform.

110
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:15,000
You can't exclude him from having a Board of Review.

111
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:22,000
That's going to ruffle some feathers and remain a debatable question, but policy is pretty clear about that.

112
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:28,000
Number eight: Rank Advancement appeals limited to Board of Review objections.

113
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:31,000
I haven't really looked at that and I'll get back to you on that.

114
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:40,000
Number nine: Eagle Scout rank application process clarified and updated and we'll talk about that in a minute in the context of talking about the Eagle Project workbook.

115
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:46,000
Number 10, Eagle Scout service project requirement changed, detailed and clarified.

116
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:56,000
There's a couple of big changes there but again I would want to expand on those in another podcast because the changes are reasonably significant.

117
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:58,000
They don't really change the spirit of much.

118
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:04,000
It's just kind of some procedural changes and I think it will be important for us to spend some time on it.

119
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:17,000
Change number 11 is new processes for requesting time extensions for earning Eagle Scout rank and that is part and parcel of the whole Eagle thing that we'll talk about in the future.

120
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:30,000
Advancement for special needs youth is clarified in change number 12 and applying for alternative requirements for a qualifying disability:

121
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:37,000
The qualifying disability need not be permanent, and that's change number 13. that's listed in the. in the guide

122
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:41,000
There are 13 what they identify as significant changes.

123
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:46,000
I'm very pleased with them and especially the clarifications in the new guide.

124
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:51,000
Some of the more debatable questions have been absolutely put to bed with this publication.

125
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:53,000
I hope

126
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:56,000
That's if everybody will take the time to read it.

127
00:12:56,000 --> 00:13:05,000
I suggest that you give the guide a complete, thoughtful read and make adjustments to your thinking and your procedures as needed.

128
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:15,000
I'm going to have to make a couple of changes of approach and that's fine, because I am 100% behind what the guide has to say.

129
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:20,000
You know, as is true with many policy statements, we need to take them at face value.

130
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:26,000
We don't need to make angels dance on the heads of pins and look at verbs and blah.

131
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:29,000
Do you understand what I'm saying?

132
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:36,000
I haven't run across anything in the guide that I thought was kind of flubbed language-wise.

133
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:38,000
It's written very, very clearly

134
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:51,000
And if you can't tell, I'm excited because I think that it will take some time for all of these changes and clarifications to promulgate themselves throughout the movement,

135
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:56,000
But once they do, it's going to solve a whole lot of difficulties.

136
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:58,000
It has that potential.

137
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:01,000
Now let's talk about the new Eagle Project workbook for a moment.

138
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:05,000
The most significant statement I've found so far is this:

139
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:08,000
It says only the official workbook may be used.

140
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:16,000
Eagle Scout candidates must use the official Eagle Scout Service Project workbook as produced by the Boy Scouts of America.

141
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:27,000
Although it is acceptable to copy and distribute the workbook, it must be maintained in the same appearance, with nothing changed, added or deleted.

142
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:39,000
No council, district, unit or individual has the authority to produce or require additional forms or to add to or change requirements

143
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:50,000
or to make any additions, deletions or changes in the text, outlines, links, graphics or other layout or informational elements of the workbook.

144
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:51,000
How about that?

145
00:14:51,000 --> 00:15:04,000
So that statement hopefully and finally puts an end to all of the unit-specific, district-specific and council-specific workbooks that are floating around out there.

146
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:18,000
I have always maintained that it's a huge disservice to Scouts who are working towards Eagle to have to jump through some of the hoops that have been set up by district and council advancement committees and unit advancement committees.

147
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:27,000
And I know that my own council advancement committee- God bless them because they're doing difficult work- in my own district advancement committee-

148
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:30,000
Again, God bless them, they're doing difficult work-

149
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:39,000
The Eagle Project approval process is sometimes a bit of a minefield, not to mention the whole Board of Review process.

150
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:42,000
People get emotional and it's difficult. so

151
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:56,000
But you know, in order to try and streamline things sometimes they have invented forms and pages and pages and pages of advice that they stick in the project workbook and you're not sure quite what is council, what's from national.

152
00:15:56,000 --> 00:16:08,000
And I have found it confusing and, you know, really kind of disheartening to try and figure out just exactly what you have to do to get a project approved.

153
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:19,000
And then you know this really weird convoluted application process that we have, at least here locally, to get an Eagle Scout application taken care of.

154
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:31,000
I think actually, if I remember correctly, I don't get directly involved in this, my advancement chair does, but if I remember correctly there's a series of like three or four forms.

155
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:39,000
You know there's an application for the application application.

156
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:51,000
But listen, you know I definitely don't want anybody to feel bad if they're a council advancement chairman or if they're a district advancement chairman and think I'm thumbing my nose at you.

157
00:16:51,000 --> 00:17:00,000
You know I know how difficult that process is and I know that, like I said, it can be a minefield at times, but I'm really happy that you know this is going to be simplified

158
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:04,000
And more uniform all the way across the board.

159
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:07,000
I will say one more thing about the new workbook that I really like.

160
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:15,000
Part of the workbook that is filled out up till the district approval thing is clearly worded as a proposal.

161
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:18,000
A proposal is different from a plan

162
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:28,000
And there is adequate space and some very good notes to help Scouts work on a plan after the district approval.

163
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:34,000
And it says very clearly in the book: this plan isn't going to be graded or anything like that.

164
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:41,000
It's just here to show you what the steps are and to help represent that you've done planning to your board of review.

165
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:54,000
And I like that because I've had in the past some run-ins with district advancement chairs who wanted to see the number of nuts and bolts that were going to be used on the project before they approved of it,

166
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:56,000
And I'm not kidding.

167
00:17:56,000 --> 00:18:00,000
So there's a clear difference now between proposal-

168
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:02,000
Hey, I'm going to build a bridge,

169
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:03,000
It's going to be at this park

170
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:05,000
And it's going to cost about this much money,

171
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:07,000
And I know where my material is coming from

172
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:09,000
And I have the approval of the organization.

173
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:14,000
And I need a conceptual proposal approval before I go any further.

174
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:16,000
I just I like that a lot better

175
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:25,000
If anybody from the advancement team from the BSA is listening. my sincere thanks for all the work you did to put this together.

176
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:27,000
I think it's a giant step forward.

177
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:40,000
You can find links to the new guide to advancement and the new Eagle project workbook in the post that contains this podcast at scoutmastercgcom,

178
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:52,000
And I'm going to all but command you to go to scoutmastercgcom or go to the bsaorg website and get these two documents.

179
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:54,000
Spend the time to read them.

180
00:18:54,000 --> 00:19:01,000
If you have questions about them, if you're wondering about them, you know I'd be more than willing to help you sort through things.

181
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:06,000
You also have plenty of resources in your district and your council to help you do that.

182
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:09,000
I don't think any of it's rocket science.

183
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:14,000
I don't think there are any major changes to the spirit of what we have been doing,

184
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:20,000
But I think a lot of things are clarified and it's going to make a lot of people sleep better and rest easier.

