and then they proceeded to have school.

204
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:56,000
As I watched, I saw several of the Scouts zone out almost immediately.

205
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:57,000
How do you fix that?

206
00:20:57,000 --> 00:21:00,000
How do we keep from having those kind of classroom sessions?

207
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:03,000
Well, Walter, I have a couple of answers for that.

208
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:05,000
First of all, just stop doing it.

209
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:09,000
That sounds trite and flippant, but I'm not being trite and flippant.

210
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:14,000
You know, I had to make a decision years ago that we would stop doing that.

211
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:20,000
We would not have groups of Scouts sitting listening to somebody teach them something.

212
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:21,000
You know what

213
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:25,000
You do, the things that Scouts do, and you instruct as you go along.

214
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:28,000
Youth leaders are much better at that than we are.

215
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:34,000
So I don't end up instructing much of anything at troop meetings or campouts or anywhere.

216
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:41,000
I end up instructing youth leaders who in turn go ahead and instruct the Scouts in the troop.

217
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:42,000
That's the way it works.

218
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:45,000
We don't do merit badges as a troop.

219
00:21:45,000 --> 00:21:47,000
We don't do them in our meetings.

220
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:49,000
We don't do them on outings.

221
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:54,000
Now some of you are going to hear that and think: well, that takes away about 90% of our program. then

222
00:21:54,000 --> 00:21:55,000
Well, no,

223
00:21:55,000 --> 00:22:01,000
Boys can challenge merit badges- any merit badge- anytime after they've joined Scouts,

224
00:22:01,000 --> 00:22:05,000
you know, and they manage to take care of it.

225
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:07,000
They'll go in groups of twos and threes.

226
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:08,000
They'll find a counselor.

227
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:09,000
They'll meet with the counselor.

228
00:22:09,000 --> 00:22:11,000
They'll do it outside of the troop meeting time,

229
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:13,000
They'll do it outside of our camping trips

230
00:22:13,000 --> 00:22:15,000
And they're very happy to do that.

231
00:22:15,000 --> 00:22:16,000
Occasionally I have a patrol.

232
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:18,000
We'll go and challenge a badge.

233
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:20,000
You know they'll find themselves a counselor.

234
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:22,000
They'll meet with a counselor during their patrol meetings

235
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:24,000
and they'll work on that badge together.

236
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:30,000
But as a matter of course we do not do any merit badge instruction in troop meetings.

237
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:34,000
We may have an introduction to a merit badge if a counselor would like to stop through

238
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:35,000
or something like that,

239
00:22:35,000 --> 00:22:38,000
But that's uncommon because most counselors come and they sit the boys down

240
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:40,000
and they start talking at them

241
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:43,000
And Scouts don't particularly care for that,

242
00:22:43,000 --> 00:22:45,000
Just like Walter didn't particularly care for that

243
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:48,000
and his boys didn't particularly care for that.

244
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:53,000
I do have a PDF file that I put together a few years ago

245
00:22:53,000 --> 00:22:55,000
about instructional methods for Scouts

246
00:22:55,000 --> 00:23:00,000
And I'll link to that in the post that contains this podcast at scoutmastercgcom.

247
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:02,000
But thanks for getting in touch, Walter, and I hope that helps.

248
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:04,000
I also heard from Crystal

249
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:06,000
And Crystal had this to say:

250
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:10,000
She says our unit is growing but we're struggling with parent participation.

251
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:14,000
It seems like every time we have a camp out, special event or anything else,

252
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:19,000
the scoutmaster who is my husband and myself are the only two adults supervising.

253
00:23:19,000 --> 00:23:22,000
We have parents that say yes, they will be there,

254
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:24,000
but often they back out at the last minute,

255
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:28,000
By the time the weekend is over. my husband and I are just exhausted.

256
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:31,000
We've tried talking to parents, sending emails, posting Facebook posts

257
00:23:31,000 --> 00:23:33,000
and making sign up sheets.

258
00:23:33,000 --> 00:23:36,000
At times my husband and I dread the idea of a camp out coming up,

259
00:23:36,000 --> 00:23:38,000
and it should not be that way.

260
00:23:38,000 --> 00:23:41,000
This month we cancelled our weekend camp out

261
00:23:41,000 --> 00:23:44,000
because we did not have enough adult participation.

262
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:46,000
Any suggestions?

263
00:23:46,000 --> 00:23:49,000
Well, last weekend two adults and myself-

264
00:23:49,000 --> 00:23:53,000
so there was three adults total- went camping with our Scouts.

265
00:23:53,000 --> 00:23:56,000
The Scouts were set up about 25 yards away from us

266
00:23:56,000 --> 00:24:00,000
and we spent all but an hour or two sitting and talking

267
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:02,000
and, you know, cooking great food

268
00:24:02,000 --> 00:24:04,000
The adults did.

269
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:06,000
The Scouts did some advancement work.

270
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:08,000
They played a lot of games,

271
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:12,000
They cooked their meals and they invited us to their campfire every evening.

272
00:24:12,000 --> 00:24:15,000
On Saturday we spent a half hour or so looking at the stars,

273
00:24:15,000 --> 00:24:17,000
while I talked about astronomy.

274
00:24:17,000 --> 00:24:21,000
But other than that, that's basically the way the weekend went,

275
00:24:21,000 --> 00:24:24,000
And it was very relaxing as an adult to be able to go

276
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:28,000
to kind of keep tabs on what was going on

277
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:32,000
down in the scout camp site,

278
00:24:32,000 --> 00:24:35,000
But they did this under the direction of their patrol leaders

279
00:24:35,000 --> 00:24:37,000
and their senior patrol leaders.

280
00:24:37,000 --> 00:24:40,000
We had very little to do.

281
00:24:40,000 --> 00:24:42,000
So my first piece of advice, Crystal,

282
00:24:42,000 --> 00:24:44,000
is to strengthen your youth leadership

283
00:24:44,000 --> 00:24:47,000
by giving them responsibility to do things for themselves.

284
00:24:47,000 --> 00:24:50,000
You might think they're too young or too inexperienced,

285
00:24:50,000 --> 00:24:53,000
but they aren't.

286
00:24:53,000 --> 00:24:55,000
They will surprise you

287
00:24:55,000 --> 00:24:59,000
and they will respond to your getting out of the way

288
00:24:59,000 --> 00:25:02,000
and letting them be responsible for what they do as scouts.

289
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:05,000
Think of your role as an observer, not a leader.

290
00:25:05,000 --> 00:25:07,000
You don't need to supervise,

291
00:25:07,000 --> 00:25:10,000
You don't need to run the show.

292
00:25:10,000 --> 00:25:13,000
Sit back and watch what happens.

293
00:25:13,000 --> 00:25:16,000
I tell adults to think of a camping trip with our boys,

294
00:25:16,000 --> 00:25:20,000
as though they're driving through one of those wild animal parks

295
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:22,000
where you get to watch the animals

296
00:25:22,000 --> 00:25:24,000
but you have to stay in the car.

297
00:25:24,000 --> 00:25:26,000
You don't get to tell the animals what to do.

298
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:28,000
They might come up to the car every once in a while

299
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:30,000
and you can be very happy that they've come

300
00:25:30,000 --> 00:25:32,000
and you get a close look at them.

301
00:25:32,000 --> 00:25:35,000
You just kind of watch and see what happens.

302
00:25:35,000 --> 00:25:38,000
Now, naturally, we're charged with the responsibility

303
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:40,000
to keep everybody safe

304
00:25:40,000 --> 00:25:43,000
and to monitor the appropriateness of the scout's activities,

305
00:25:43,000 --> 00:25:46,000
But we can do this at more of a distance than you may think,

306
00:25:46,000 --> 00:25:49,000
and we can do it without interfering with their plans,

307
00:25:49,000 --> 00:25:51,000
And they do need to have plans.

308
00:25:51,000 --> 00:25:53,000
Now, prior to the weekend,

309
00:25:53,000 --> 00:25:56,000
I asked my senior patrol leader what they had planned

310
00:25:56,000 --> 00:25:58,000
He told me. He said they were going to work on advancement.

311
00:25:58,000 --> 00:26:00,000
They're going to play some games.

312
00:26:00,000 --> 00:26:03,000
I asked if he needed any help or support from the adult leaders.

313
00:26:03,000 --> 00:26:05,000
He said no,

314
00:26:05,000 --> 00:26:07,000
And so we sat and watched them from a distance,

315
00:26:07,000 --> 00:26:09,000
just like we were in that wild animal park.

316
00:26:09,000 --> 00:26:12,000
Now, had any safety or behavioral issues arose,

317
00:26:12,000 --> 00:26:16,000
they would have tried to resolve them themselves first

318
00:26:16,000 --> 00:26:18,000
And, if they had, if they couldn't,

319
00:26:18,000 --> 00:26:20,000
they would have called on us for our help.

320
00:26:20,000 --> 00:26:23,000
And this all may sound a little impossible,

321
00:26:23,000 --> 00:26:25,000
a little far-fetched and a little scary,

322
00:26:25,000 --> 00:26:27,000
but I've got to tell you it works.

323
00:26:27,000 --> 00:26:31,000
They did not do everything like I would have done it.

324
00:26:31,000 --> 00:26:33,000
They didn't instruct as well as I can.

325
00:26:33,000 --> 00:26:35,000
They don't cook as well as I can.

326
00:26:35,000 --> 00:26:37,000
They don't set up tents as well as I can.

327
00:26:37,000 --> 00:26:39,000
They don't lead as well as I can.

328
00:26:39,000 --> 00:26:42,000
I've got 30 years of scout leader experience behind me.

329
00:26:42,000 --> 00:26:45,000
I'm older, I'm smarter, You know.

330
00:26:45,000 --> 00:26:47,000
But they're doing it for themselves

331
00:26:47,000 --> 00:26:49,000
and that's what's really important.

332
00:26:49,000 --> 00:26:51,000
Finally, I'd say, Crystal, if you make up for the lack of help

333
00:26:51,000 --> 00:26:54,000
by doing it all yourselves, nobody's ever going to notice.

334
00:26:54,000 --> 00:26:56,000
They're going to think everything is fine.

335
00:26:56,000 --> 00:26:58,000
If you cancel trips and activities

336
00:26:58,000 --> 00:27:00,000
because there's insufficient adult coverage,

337
00:27:00,000 --> 00:27:02,000
that's really a matter for the troop committee,

338
00:27:02,000 --> 00:27:04,000
not the scout master.

339
00:27:04,000 --> 00:27:06,000
The troop committee needs to back you up

340
00:27:06,000 --> 00:27:09,000
to make arrangements to have adequate adult participation.

341
00:27:09,000 --> 00:27:11,000
They need to know that this is their job

342
00:27:11,000 --> 00:27:14,000
and that you will no longer be doing it for them.

343
00:27:14,000 --> 00:27:16,000
Otherwise there's not a whole lot of hope

344
00:27:16,000 --> 00:27:18,000
that that situation will change.

345
00:27:18,000 --> 00:27:20,000
Some of these things take some time to fix.

346
00:27:20,000 --> 00:27:22,000
Some of these things take some time to happen.

347
00:27:22,000 --> 00:27:24,000
but you have to be patient

348
00:27:24,000 --> 00:27:27,000
and you have to just assiduously apply

349
00:27:27,000 --> 00:27:29,000
the principles that I'm talking about.

350
00:27:29,000 --> 00:27:32,000
Let boys do the thing that they need to do,

351
00:27:32,000 --> 00:27:34,000
Get the troop committee to start backing you up

352
00:27:34,000 --> 00:27:38,000
and you'll start to see these problems kind of dissolve.

353
00:27:38,000 --> 00:27:41,000
Thanks for listening and thanks for reading, Crystal,

354
00:27:41,000 --> 00:27:44,000
and thanks for being in touch, and I hope that helps.

355
00:27:44,000 --> 00:27:49,000
The third email came to me reasonably anonymously

356
00:27:49,000 --> 00:27:51,000
and so I'm going to keep it that way.

357
00:27:51,000 --> 00:27:53,000
It says this: I'm a relatively new

358
00:27:53,000 --> 00:27:55,000
and relatively young scout master.

359
00:27:55,000 --> 00:27:57,000
I'm in my mid-20s.

360
00:27:57,000 --> 00:28:00,000
I have a parent who is also one of my assistant scout masters.

361
00:28:00,000 --> 00:28:03,000
They have two boys in the troop, 13 and 15,

362
00:28:03,000 --> 00:28:05,000
both of whom act spoiled, selfish

363
00:28:05,000 --> 00:28:07,000
and expect to be given badges and advancements

364
00:28:07,000 --> 00:28:09,000
after very little effort.

365
00:28:09,000 --> 00:28:11,000
They throw tantrums when things don't go their way

366
00:28:11,000 --> 00:28:13,000
and they are the first ones to disappear

367
00:28:13,000 --> 00:28:15,000
when work needs to be done.

368
00:28:15,000 --> 00:28:17,000
Recently, one of the boys came to

369
00:28:17,000 --> 00:28:20,000
a scout master's conference with me.

370
00:28:20,000 --> 00:28:22,000
He approached the conference with little effort

371
00:28:22,000 --> 00:28:24,000
into his uniform.

372
00:28:24,000 --> 00:28:27,000
He expressed very little enthusiasm to advance.

373
00:28:27,000 --> 00:28:29,000
In fact he stated that his mom was making him

374
00:28:29,000 --> 00:28:31,000
do the conference and then he was enabled

375
00:28:31,000 --> 00:28:33,000
to answer any of the questions I asked him

376
00:28:33,000 --> 00:28:35,000
about requirements.

377
00:28:35,000 --> 00:28:37,000
Because of this, I did not sign the requirement

378
00:28:37,000 --> 00:28:39,000
for the scout master's conference.

379
00:28:39,000 --> 00:28:41,000
Now I did remain positive

380
00:28:41,000 --> 00:28:43,000
and I did not admonish the scout at all.

381
00:28:43,000 --> 00:28:45,000
Instead, I encouraged him to learn more

382
00:28:45,000 --> 00:28:47,000
and challenged him to be the great scout

383
00:28:47,000 --> 00:28:49,000
I know he can be.

384
00:28:49,000 --> 00:28:51,000
After the conference I was approached

385
00:28:51,000 --> 00:28:53,000
by his dad, the assistant scout master,

386
00:28:53,000 --> 00:28:55,000
and I was given an earful of accusations,

387
00:28:55,000 --> 00:28:57,000
questions and condescension

388
00:28:57,000 --> 00:28:59,000
for not having signed off the scout's conference.

389
00:28:59,000 --> 00:29:01,000
It became clear to me that this scout

390
00:29:01,000 --> 00:29:03,000
doesn't hear the word no very often.

391
00:29:03,000 --> 00:29:05,000
How should I deal with this situation?

392
00:29:05,000 --> 00:29:07,000
I started as a scout master

393
00:29:07,000 --> 00:29:09,000
at the age of 24..

394
00:29:09,000 --> 00:29:11,000
That's 27 years ago.

395
00:29:11,000 --> 00:29:13,000
I've had my share of difficult scouts

396
00:29:13,000 --> 00:29:15,000
and I've experienced the same frustrations

397
00:29:15,000 --> 00:29:17,000
over and over again.

398
00:29:17,000 --> 00:29:19,000
So I'm going to answer this

399
00:29:19,000 --> 00:29:21,000
question plainly.

400
00:29:21,000 --> 00:29:23,000
Not because I think I know everything.

401
00:29:23,000 --> 00:29:25,000
I've been around a little longer,

402
00:29:25,000 --> 00:29:27,000
I have more experience. I'm not smarter

403
00:29:27,000 --> 00:29:29,000
than you.

404
00:29:29,000 --> 00:29:31,000
The language you use

405
00:29:31,000 --> 00:29:33,000
in describing these two scouts

406
00:29:33,000 --> 00:29:35,000
tells me you've already judged them

407
00:29:35,000 --> 00:29:37,000
and that they're unworthy of your attention

408
00:29:37,000 --> 00:29:39,000
or approval. You're calling them spoiled

409
00:29:39,000 --> 00:29:41,000
and selfish and they throw tantrums

410
00:29:41,000 --> 00:29:43,000
and everything like that.

411
00:29:43,000 --> 00:29:45,000
That might be a sober assessment

412
00:29:45,000 --> 00:29:47,000
of their behavior,

413
00:29:47,000 --> 00:29:49,000
but

414
00:29:49,000 --> 00:29:51,000
it indicates to me

415
00:29:51,000 --> 00:29:53,000
that this is really not their problem.

416
00:29:53,000 --> 00:29:55,000
It's your problem as a scout master.

417
00:29:55,000 --> 00:29:57,000
It's up to you to rise above a lot of the things

418
00:29:57,000 --> 00:29:59,000
that frustrate you

419
00:29:59,000 --> 00:30:01,000
and find the best in every scout.

420
00:30:01,000 --> 00:30:03,000
You may not like those scouts.

421
00:30:03,000 --> 00:30:05,000
You may not like them as individuals,

422
00:30:05,000 --> 00:30:07,000
You may not like their parents

423
00:30:07,000 --> 00:30:09,000
and you may not like the way they choose

424
00:30:09,000 --> 00:30:11,000
to raise their children,

425
00:30:11,000 --> 00:30:13,000
but it's really none of your business.

426
00:30:13,000 --> 00:30:15,000
Your job as a scout master

427
00:30:15,000 --> 00:30:17,000
is to meet them where they are

428
00:30:17,000 --> 00:30:19,000
and encourage them to go farther

429
00:30:19,000 --> 00:30:21,000
at their own speed and to the best

430
00:30:21,000 --> 00:30:23,000
of their individual abilities.

431
00:30:23,000 --> 00:30:25,000
You may hear and see plenty of things

432
00:30:25,000 --> 00:30:27,000
about scouts and their families.

433
00:30:27,000 --> 00:30:29,000
that's going to rub you the wrong way,

434
00:30:29,000 --> 00:30:31,000
Unless the problems you see

435
00:30:31,000 --> 00:30:33,000
rise to the level of abuse.

436
00:30:33,000 --> 00:30:35,000
in a legal sense

437
00:30:35,000 --> 00:30:37,000
it's really pretty much none of your business.

438
00:30:37,000 --> 00:30:39,000
If you try to make it your business,

439
00:30:39,000 --> 00:30:41,000
you'll make an enemy of the scout and his parents.

440
00:30:41,000 --> 00:30:43,000
I've learned nobody can look

441
00:30:43,000 --> 00:30:45,000
into another person's home

442
00:30:45,000 --> 00:30:47,000
or into another person's family

443
00:30:47,000 --> 00:30:49,000
and truly understand what's going on.

444
00:30:49,000 --> 00:30:51,000
The other thing is spoiled, selfish scouts

445
00:30:51,000 --> 00:30:53,000
are a dime, a dozen, For Pete's sake.

446
00:30:53,000 --> 00:30:55,000
they're 13 and 15 years old.

447
00:30:55,000 --> 00:30:57,000
What is the definition of spoiled

448
00:30:57,000 --> 00:30:59,000
and selfish being 13 to 15 years old?

449
00:30:59,000 --> 00:31:01,000
They're still making their way

450
00:31:01,000 --> 00:31:03,000
in the world, but you have a chance

451
00:31:03,000 --> 00:31:05,000
as their scout master to influence them

452
00:31:05,000 --> 00:31:07,000
for the good. You've got to adjust

453
00:31:07,000 --> 00:31:09,000
your attitude so that you can do so.

454
00:31:09,000 --> 00:31:11,000
I also want to talk about the matter

455
00:31:11,000 --> 00:31:13,000
of not signing the requirement

456
00:31:13,000 --> 00:31:15,000
for the scout master's conference.

457
00:31:15,000 --> 00:31:17,000
Now I have probably done close to

458
00:31:17,000 --> 00:31:19,000
1,000 scout master conferences

459
00:31:19,000 --> 00:31:21,000
and I think maybe

460
00:31:21,000 --> 00:31:23,000
five of those ended

461
00:31:23,000 --> 00:31:25,000
in an agreement with the scout that he was not ready

462
00:31:25,000 --> 00:31:27,000
to advance.

463
00:31:27,000 --> 00:31:29,000
In those

464
00:31:29,000 --> 00:31:31,000
very rare instances the scout

465
00:31:31,000 --> 00:31:33,000
left the conference with a clear

466
00:31:33,000 --> 00:31:35,000
understanding of specifically what

467
00:31:35,000 --> 00:31:37,000
he needed to do to advance

468
00:31:37,000 --> 00:31:39,000
and when we would resume the conference.

469
00:31:39,000 --> 00:31:41,000
He left

470
00:31:41,000 --> 00:31:43,000
happy. he left energized

471
00:31:43,000 --> 00:31:45,000
and knowing that he could take care of it

472
00:31:45,000 --> 00:31:47,000
and things would be fine.

473
00:31:47,000 --> 00:31:49,000
I didn't ding him because he didn't

474
00:31:49,000 --> 00:31:51,000
have his uniform arranged properly.

475
00:31:51,000 --> 00:31:53,000
I didn't ding him

476
00:31:53,000 --> 00:31:55,000
because he seemed a little

477
00:31:55,000 --> 00:31:57,000
lackluster. I didn't ding him

478
00:31:57,000 --> 00:31:59,000
because he said his mom forced him

479
00:31:59,000 --> 00:32:01,000
to come to the conference.

480
00:32:01,000 --> 00:32:03,000
What I would do is I would get

481
00:32:03,000 --> 00:32:05,000
on his side. I would just start

482
00:32:05,000 --> 00:32:07,000
asking questions and talking about things

483
00:32:07,000 --> 00:32:09,000
and finding a way out of

484
00:32:09,000 --> 00:32:11,000
what was obviously a difficult situation.

485
00:32:11,000 --> 00:32:13,000
Now I assume that

486
00:32:13,000 --> 00:32:15,000
you're asking questions about requirements

487
00:32:15,000 --> 00:32:17,000
of the scout master conference

488
00:32:17,000 --> 00:32:19,000
only to sign some off that

489
00:32:19,000 --> 00:32:21,000
haven't been signed off yet,

490
00:32:21,000 --> 00:32:23,000
and we are never retesting

491
00:32:23,000 --> 00:32:25,000
requirements when we have

492
00:32:25,000 --> 00:32:27,000
scout master conferences, right?

493
00:32:27,000 --> 00:32:29,000
I think we need to make sure that

494
00:32:29,000 --> 00:32:31,000
that's being understood.

495
00:32:31,000 --> 00:32:33,000
As scout masters, we need

496
00:32:33,000 --> 00:32:35,000
to be totally unreservedly

497
00:32:35,000 --> 00:32:37,000
and completely invested in one thing

498
00:32:37,000 --> 00:32:39,000
and one thing only, and that's the success

499
00:32:39,000 --> 00:32:41,000
of our scouts.

500
00:32:41,000 --> 00:32:43,000
We need to do our best to leave

501
00:32:43,000 --> 00:32:45,000
and their parents at the door

502
00:32:45,000 --> 00:32:47,000
and work with each one as an individual,

503
00:32:47,000 --> 00:32:49,000
never in the context

504
00:32:49,000 --> 00:32:51,000
of who his parents are

505
00:32:51,000 --> 00:32:53,000
or what we think of their home.

506
00:32:53,000 --> 00:32:55,000
This takes a lot of effort.

507
00:32:55,000 --> 00:32:57,000
Sometimes it's galling,

508
00:32:57,000 --> 00:32:59,000
sometimes it's difficult,

509
00:32:59,000 --> 00:33:01,000
but really that's the heart of our job:

510
00:33:01,000 --> 00:33:03,000
the success of our scouts.

511
00:33:03,000 --> 00:33:05,000
So let's say you have

512
00:33:05,000 --> 00:33:07,000
a poorly behaved scout.

513
00:33:07,000 --> 00:33:09,000
He comes to you

514
00:33:09,000 --> 00:33:11,000
to have a scout masters conference.

515
00:33:11,000 --> 00:33:13,000
You sit down, you get on his side,

516
00:33:13,000 --> 00:33:15,000
you work with him, you talk with him,

517
00:33:15,000 --> 00:33:17,000
you identify the things that he needs to do

518
00:33:17,000 --> 00:33:19,000
to satisfy the requirements,

519
00:33:19,000 --> 00:33:21,000
to maybe correct a couple

520
00:33:21,000 --> 00:33:23,000
of things about behavior

521
00:33:23,000 --> 00:33:25,000
or attitude that are going to help him

522
00:33:25,000 --> 00:33:27,000
in the future. and then you say

523
00:33:27,000 --> 00:33:29,000
we're going to resume our scout master conference next week

524
00:33:29,000 --> 00:33:31,000
after you've had some time to think about this.

525
00:33:31,000 --> 00:33:33,000
Now is that okay with you?

526
00:33:33,000 --> 00:33:35,000
Do you think that that's a smart thing for us to do

527
00:33:35,000 --> 00:33:37,000
And get him

528
00:33:37,000 --> 00:33:39,000
on your side

529
00:33:39,000 --> 00:33:41,000
and work together

530
00:33:41,000 --> 00:33:43,000
to solve these problems bit by bit?

531
00:33:43,000 --> 00:33:45,000
And once you've done that

532
00:33:45,000 --> 00:33:47,000
and the boy leaves the conference

533
00:33:47,000 --> 00:33:49,000
and he knows what he needs to do-

534
00:33:49,000 --> 00:33:51,000
he might not be thoroughly happy,

535
00:33:51,000 --> 00:33:53,000
but he needs to be because of your attitude

536
00:33:53,000 --> 00:33:55,000
and the way that you've spoken with him.

537
00:33:55,000 --> 00:33:57,000
he needs to be energized

538
00:33:57,000 --> 00:33:59,000
and he needs to be thinking forward

539
00:33:59,000 --> 00:34:01,000
and not resenting you

540
00:34:01,000 --> 00:34:03,000
and not thinking that he's

541
00:34:03,000 --> 00:34:05,000
some kind of failure.

542
00:34:05,000 --> 00:34:07,000
Once that has happened

543
00:34:07,000 --> 00:34:09,000
or throws a tantrum,

544
00:34:09,000 --> 00:34:11,000
then you just ask to have

545
00:34:11,000 --> 00:34:13,000
their scout show the specific things

546
00:34:13,000 --> 00:34:15,000
that you both agreed he needed to work on,

547
00:34:15,000 --> 00:34:17,000
Talk about when the conference will resume,

548
00:34:17,000 --> 00:34:19,000
and

549
00:34:19,000 --> 00:34:21,000
if that doesn't do it for them,

550
00:34:21,000 --> 00:34:23,000
you say: well, look, the next time we sit down

551
00:34:23,000 --> 00:34:25,000
and have a conference you are welcome to observe.

552
00:34:25,000 --> 00:34:27,000
You can sit down

553
00:34:27,000 --> 00:34:29,000
and listen to me, talk to your son

554
00:34:29,000 --> 00:34:31,000
and hopefully you'll have a good idea

555
00:34:31,000 --> 00:34:33,000
that I'm on his side and we're trying to make things better here.

556
00:34:33,000 --> 00:34:35,000
I know you may be disappointed

557
00:34:35,000 --> 00:34:37,000
right now that he didn't move forward tonight,

558
00:34:37,000 --> 00:34:39,000
but this is going to

559
00:34:39,000 --> 00:34:41,000
all get resolved

560
00:34:41,000 --> 00:34:43,000
and all I'm asking for you to do

561
00:34:43,000 --> 00:34:45,000
is to have a little bit of patience.

562
00:34:45,000 --> 00:34:47,000
That will take care

563
00:34:47,000 --> 00:34:49,000
of these very rare instances

564
00:34:49,000 --> 00:34:51,000
where you have to turn a boy away

565
00:34:51,000 --> 00:34:53,000
from a Scout Masters conference

566
00:34:53,000 --> 00:34:55,000
for not having completed it.

567
00:34:55,000 --> 00:34:57,000
My sense, my friend,

568
00:34:57,000 --> 00:34:59,000
in sending me this email,

569
00:34:59,000 --> 00:35:01,000
some of the language you used

570
00:35:01,000 --> 00:35:03,000
and just the general tone of it-

571
00:35:03,000 --> 00:35:05,000
you're pretty disgusted with these couple of scouts.

572
00:35:05,000 --> 00:35:07,000
That's not their problem,

573
00:35:07,000 --> 00:35:09,000
That's your problem.

574
00:35:09,000 --> 00:35:11,000
There's ways to work it. There's ways to get around it.

575
00:35:11,000 --> 00:35:13,000
You need to do a little thinking

576
00:35:13,000 --> 00:35:15,000
and it'll make you feel

577
00:35:15,000 --> 00:35:17,000
a whole lot better in the end.

